        "title": "Statistical Mechanics - Landscape and Taxonomy",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Statistical mechanics is the bridge between microscopic laws (Newtonian or quantum mechanics of individual particles) and macroscopic observables (temperature, pressure, entropy, heat capacity). The central insight is that macroscopic behavior emerges from statistical properties of an astronomically large number (N ~ 10²³) of microscopic degrees of freedom. You don't need to know the trajectory of every molecule — only the probability distribution over microstates. The partition function is the key object: it encodes all thermodynamic information."
